$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Typical Procedure for the Palladium-Catalyzed Cross-Coupling Reaction of
1 with Aryl Iodide (Scheme 3; 1 → 3, Condition A). To a solution of
PdCl2(CH3CN)2 (4.1 mg, 0.016 mmol, 5 mol %) and P(C6F5)3 (16.9 mg, 0.032
mmol, 10 mol %) in THF (1.0 mL) were added 1 (145.0 mg, 0.33 mmol),
4-iodoacetophenone (2b: 93.6 mg, 0.38 mmol), and THF (1.0 mL). After the
mixture was stirred at 50 °C for 24 h, 1,8-diazabicyclo[5.4.0]undec-7-ene
(ca. 0.1 mL) and Et2O (1.0 mL) were added to the mixture. The catalyst
and tin residue were removed by filtration through a short silica gel pad
(EtOAc). The filtrate was evaporated to give the crude product. Benzylic
silane 3b was isolated in a pure form by the acid-base extraction as
follows. The solution of crude mixture in Et2O (2 mL) was extracted with
1 N aq HCl (5 × 1 mL). The combined aqueous phase was neutralized by
adding NaHCO3 and then was extracted with EtOAc (3 × 20 mL). Drying over
MgSO4 and removal of solvents under reduced pressure afforded 3b (72.7
mg, 82%) as pale yellow oil. This procedure (condition A) was applied to
the synthesis of 3b, 3d, 3e, and 3f. The alternative procedure (condition
B), where P(C6H4CF3-4)3 was used instead of P(C6F5)3 and the reaction
temperature was raised to 70 °C, was applied for the synthesis of 3a.
Compound 3b, yield (72.7 mg, 82%). 1H NMR (300 MHz) δ 0.31 (s, 6H), 2.51
(s, 2H), 2.54 (s, 3H), 7.00 (d, J = 8.1 Hz, 2H), 7.25 (ddd, J = 7.5, 4.8,
1.2 Hz, 1H), 7.38 (dt, J = 7.5, 1.2 Hz, 1H), 7.59 (td, J = 7.5, 1.2 Hz,
1H), 7.76 (dt, J = 8.4, 1.8 Hz, 2H), 8.81 (ddd, J = 4.8, 1.5, 1.2 Hz,
1H); 13C NMR (75 MHz) δ -4.1, 25.8, 26.4, 123.1, 128.2, 128.4, 129.4,
133.4, 134.1, 146.3, 150.1, 165.7, 197.8. HRMS (EI) m/z calcd for
C16H19NOSi: 269.1236, found 269.1232. Anal. Calcd for C16H19NOSi: C,
71.33; H, 7.11; N, 5.20. Found: C, 71.16; H, 7.13; N, 5.11.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM anaerobic, 3rd and 4th stages are acid-base extn. which allows simple
isolation of product
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM A General and Straightforward Route toward Diarylmethanes. Integrated
Cross-Coupling Reactions Using (2-Pyridyl)silylmethylstannane as an
Air-Stable, Storable, and Versatile Coupling Platform
